      <h2>3. The 7th CPC Slashed Rates & The 50% DA Restoration Trigger</h2>
      <p>
        The transition under the 7th CPC in 2016 caused significant dissatisfaction regarding housing compensation. Historically, under the 6th CPC, HRA was disbursed at <strong>30% (Class X), 20% (Class Y), and 10% (Class Z)</strong>.
      </p>
      <p>
        However, the 7th Pay Commission observed that because basic salaries were scaling upward by 2.57x, maintaining 30%, 20%, and 10% would create an unsustainable expenditure spike for the union budget. Consequently, the commission controversially slashed initial HRA base rates down to:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Class X:</strong> 24% of Basic Pay</li>
        <li><strong>Class Y:</strong> 16% of Basic Pay</li>
        <li><strong>Class Z:</strong> 8% of Basic Pay</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        To prevent permanent wage suppression, the government introduced an automated, two-stage inflation restoration trigger:
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Trigger Stage 1 (DA crosses 25%):</strong> HRA rates adjusted upward to <strong>27%, 18%, and 9%</strong>.</li>
        <li><strong>Trigger Stage 2 (DA crosses 50%):</strong> HRA rates fully restored to their historical benchmarks of <strong>30%, 20%, and 10%</strong>.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        With Dearness Allowance officially surpassing 50% under the 7th CPC, employees currently receive the restored 30%, 20%, and 10% rates. <strong>The core demand for the 8th Pay Commission</strong> is establishing 30%, 20%, and 10% as the permanent baseline floor from day one, rather than resetting allowances downward to reduced percentages when accumulated DA is absorbed into basic pay.
      </p>

      <div class="formula-banner">
        Projected 8th CPC Monthly HRA = Revised 8th CPC Basic Pay × City Tier Percentage (30%, 20%, or 10%)
      </div>

