      <h2>5. The Minimum HRA Floor Protection Rule</h2>
      <p>
        A key administrative safeguard in the Central Civil Services (Allowance) Rules is the <strong>Statutory Minimum Floor Protection</strong>. This policy ensures that entry-level personnel stationed in expensive metropolitan or urban centers do not receive housing allowances that fall below the minimum threshold required to secure basic rental shelter.
      </p>
      <p>
        Regardless of how low an employee's starting basic pay might be, their monthly HRA cannot drop below the established floor:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>7th CPC Floor Limits:</strong> ₹5,400 per month (Class X), ₹3,600 per month (Class Y), and ₹1,800 per month (Class Z).</li>
        <li><strong>Projected 8th CPC Floor Limits:</strong> Applying the standard 2.25x to 2.86x fitment expansion, the minimum guaranteed floors are projected to recalibrate to approximately <strong>₹12,150 – ₹15,444</strong> (Class X), <strong>₹8,100 – ₹10,296</strong> (Class Y), and <strong>₹4,050 – ₹5,148</strong> (Class Z).</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        If the mathematical percentage of basic pay yields an amount lower than the statutory floor, the Accounts Office must disburse the full minimum floor amount automatically.
      </p>

      <h2>6. Income Tax Implications: Claiming Section 10(13A) Exemption</h2>
      <p>
        Unlike allowances that are fully taxable or fully exempt, the tax treatment of House Rent Allowance depends heavily on residency conditions and tax regime selections:
      </p>

      <h3>1. Living in Self-Owned or Non-Rented Family Housing</h3>
      <p>
        If a government employee resides in their own house, or stays in ancestral family property without paying verifiable commercial rent, the entire HRA drawn on their monthly pay slip is treated as taxable salary income.
      </p>

      <h3>2. Living in Private Rented Accommodations (Old Tax Regime Exemption)</h3>
      <p>
        Employees opting for the Old Tax Regime can claim substantial exemptions under <strong>Section 10(13A) of the Income Tax Act</strong>, read in conjunction with Rule 2A. The exempt amount is legally calculated as the <em>least</em> of the following three variables:
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li>The total actual HRA amount received from the government during the financial year.</li>
        <li>Actual annual rent paid minus 10% of annual Basic Salary (including Dearness Allowance).</li>
        <li>50% of Basic Salary for employees residing in Delhi, Mumbai, Kolkata, or Chennai (40% for all other non-metro locations).</li>
      </ol>

      <div class="formula-banner">
        Compliance Requirement: If annual rent paid exceeds ₹1,00,000, furnishing the landlord's Permanent Account Number (PAN) to the Drawing & Disbursing Officer (DDO) is mandatory.
      </div>

      <h3>3. The New Tax Regime Dilemma</h3>
      <p>
        Under the simplified <strong>New Tax Regime</strong> (which serves as the statutory default since FY 2023–24), Section 10(13A) HRA exemptions are <strong>completely disallowed</strong>. Government servants drawing substantially expanded 8th CPC housing allowances must evaluate whether the lower slab tax rates of the New Regime offset the loss of housing exemptions, standard deductions, and Section 80C allowances under the Old Regime.
      </p>

      <h2>7. Dual Government Employee Spouses & Allotment Regulations</h2>
      <p>
        Specific statutory provisions govern households with multiple public servants:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Government Accommodation (General Pool Residential Accommodation - GPRA):</strong> When a government servant occupies government-allotted quarters, they forfeit their entire monthly HRA entitlement and must pay a nominal license fee.</li>
        <li><strong>The Dual-Spouse Restriction:</strong> If both husband and wife are government employees (central, state, or autonomous bodies) stationed at the same station, and one spouse accepts government quarters, the other spouse is legally debarred from drawing HRA, even if they maintain separate living quarters.</li>
        <li><strong>Private Rented Living:</strong> If both spouses reside in a private rented home, only one spouse is permitted to claim HRA disbursement and submit rent receipts for Section 10(13A) tax relief.</li>
      </ul>

